-- title:
--   Definitions for The quantum dynamic capacity formula of a quantum channel
-- statement:
--   The objects the formalized statements of *The quantum dynamic capacity formula of a quantum channel* (arXiv:1004.0458) are stated in. Each was checked against the paper by a blind read-back and a moderator.
--
--   - `Rate`: PDF pp.3--6: coordinates 0,1,2 are C,Q,E, with their usual Euclidean topology.
--   - `unitCone`: PDF p.7: exactly the cone generated by ED, superdense coding, and teleportation.
--   - `DynamicPureEnsemble`: PDF pp.6,11: an arbitrary finite reference and a finite normalized pure ensemble. Neither dimension is fixed by the channel or by a preceding ensemble.
--   - `pureOutputs`: PDF p.6 (12): channel output on each pure input, preserving its reference.
--   - `cqHolevoBits`: PDF pp.3,11 (18): Holevo information of normalized conditional bipartite outputs.
--   - `jointMutual`: PDF pp.3,11 (19): I(RX;B); classical entropy terms cancel exactly.
--   - `conditionalCoherent`: PDF pp.3,11 (20): I(R>B X)=sum_x p_x [H(B)_x-H(RB)_x].
--   - `bounds`: PDF p.6 (9)--(11): the three constraint right sides on ONE ensemble.
--   - `rateConstraints`: PDF p.6: all three inequalities use the same right-side triple.
--   - `oneShotRegion`: PDF p.6: unclosed one-shot union over all finite pure ensembles and references.
--   - `regularizedRegion`: PDF p.6 (8): closure OUTSIDE the union; positive blocklength and inverse scaling.
--   - `dynamicScore`: PDF p.11 (21): scalarization on one common ensemble, including the entire mu term.
--   - `dynamicValues`: PDF p.11 (21): feasible values, with normalized states even at zero-weight labels.
--   - `dynamic`: PDF p.11 (21): D. For CPTP N on nonempty finite systems and nonnegative weights this set is nonempty, bounded by output-dimension entropy bounds, and attains its supremum. Extra reference dimensions cannot make these values unbounded.
--   - `regularizedDynamic`: PDF p.11, Definition 4: positive-block supremum implementation of Dreg. The value set is nonempty and bounded for finite CPTP channels. Lemma 1 below explicitly asserts convergence to this value, so no default real limit is used.
--   - `AdditiveDynamic`: PDF p.12, Lemma 1: genuinely GLOBAL pairwise additivity, not just N tensor N.
--   - `SingleLetterDynamic`: PDF p.12: single-letterization includes a genuine limit at every nonnegative weight.
--   - `pareto`: PDF p.11: componentwise maximal points, not the full topological boundary.
--   - `Resources`: PDF pp.4--6: six independent positive resource dimensions. Coordinates are (Cgen,Qgen,Egen,Ccons,Qcons,Econs), so dimension one means zero gross resource.
--   - `EncoderInput`: PDF p.4: the local encoder's M,A1,TA input.
--   - `EncoderOutput`: PDF pp.4--5: noisy block input, SA, classical L, and noiseless quantum A2.
--   - `DecoderInput`: PDF p.5: decoder has exactly B^n,A2,TB,L and no feedback register.
--   - `DecoderOutput`: PDF p.5: decoder returns B1,SB,Mhat.
--   - `FinalRegister`: PDF p.5 (4): joint retained marginal ordered as (M,Mhat),(R,B1),(SA,SB).
--   - `DynamicCode`: PDF pp.4--6: physical catalytic protocol. Local environments and randomness are included in the arbitrary CPTP maps; no entropic constraint defines the code.
--   - `initialBlock`: PDF pp.4--5: an unnormalized encoder block with untouched external M,R,TB indices. Normalization is applied once in catalyticFinalState, from the initial three resources.
--   - `catalyticFinalState`: PDF p.5: actual final M,Mhat,R,B1,SA,SB marginal, by matrix-unit expansion. The L wire is dephased before Bob receives it; Mhat is read out classically. A2 and TB remain fully quantum. Both local maps are fixed before the uniformly distributed message and reference. The prefactor is 1/(|M| |A1| |TA|), from classical correlation and two Bell states.
--   - `idealFinal`: PDF p.5 (4): uniform classical correlation tensor Bell(R,B1) tensor Bell(SA,SB). Its denominator differs from the initial state's denominator when entanglement is generated.
--   - `catalyticJointError`: PDF p.5 (4): FULL trace norm of the entire joint marginal error.
--   - `grossRate`: PDF pp.5--6: integer resource dimensions divided by a positive blocklength. Only n>=1 is used by achievability; there is no artificial exact-real dimension constraint.
--   - `netRates`: PDF p.6 (5)--(7): signed net rates, generation minus consumption.
--   - `AchievableDynamicRates`: PDF p.6, Definition 1, with usual integer rounding made explicit. Finite gross rates are fixed BEFORE both tolerances and every blocklength. Every sufficiently large positive n has a code, not just a favorable subsequence.
--   - `operationalDynamicRegion`: PDF p.6, Definition 2: independently defined operational capacity region.
--   - `singleMutualValues`: PDF p.14: mutual-information values over arbitrary reference purifications.
--   - `singleCoherentValues`: PDF p.14: coherent-information values of arbitrary pure bipartite inputs.
--   - `cqMutualValues`: PDF p.14, Corollary 1: all cq mutual-information values.
--   - `cqCoherentValues`: PDF p.14, Corollary 2: all cq conditional coherent-information values.
--   - `cqSumValues`: PDF pp.14--15, Corollary 3: optimize the SUM on one common cq ensemble.
--   - `pureHolevoValues`: PDF pp.14--15: pure, unentangled channel-input ensembles on the HSW side.
--   - `dephasingGamma`: PDF p.16: gamma contains p/2 because p is complete-dephasing probability.
--   - `dephasingBounds`: PDF p.16 (26)--(28): one common nu for all three constraints.
--   - `dephasingVector`: PDF pp.16--17 (29)--(31): canonical purifications of the two displayed marginals.
--   - `dephasingBinaryScore`: PDF pp.16--18: actual scalar functional of the equiprobable binary ensemble (29). On 0<=nu<=1/2 its vectors are normalized; reference isometries leave the score invariant.
--   - `erasureBounds`: PDF p.18, Theorem 5: erasure bounds, with a shared scalar p.
--   - `erasureScore`: PDF pp.20--21 (34): the entire scalar maximand, without a coefficient restriction.
--   - `erasureCoefficient`: PDF p.21: sign-sensitive entropy coefficient in the erasure maximand.
--   - `erasureCEF`: PDF pp.18--19: CEF endpoints, parametrized by input entropy h=0 or 1. The Q coordinate is (1-epsilon)*h and E is -epsilon*h, including full erasure.
--   - `classicalBranchRegion`: PDF p.21: the classical endpoint translated by the unit-resource cone.
-- source:
--   The quantum dynamic capacity formula of a quantum channel, arXiv:1004.0458v3, https://arxiv.org/abs/1004.0458v3

